Subject: the other null-pointer sizeof, in the xattr block loop

0003 replaced this idiom in ext4_xattr_list()'s in-inode loop. There are
two copies of it: the function lists the entries in the inode body, then
the entries in the xattr block, and each loop sizes its buffer the same
way. The second one was missed, and stayed missed, because nothing in the
test corpus ever reached it.

Reaching it needs a volume whose xattrs live in a block rather than in the
inode -- which is what 128-byte inodes give you, since there is no extra
inode space for an attribute at all. Every fixture in this project until
now used 256-byte inodes, so every attribute fitted in the inode body and
the block loop never ran with an entry in it.

    Core/lwext4/src/ext4_xattr.c:995:51: runtime error: applying non-zero
    offset 32 to null pointer

Found by fuzzing, on a pristine e2fsck-clean seed rather than on a mutated
one: seed s10, mke2fs -I 128, listxattr on a file with a 700-byte value.
The generated code is the same either way; sizeof is what it means.

--- a/src/ext4_xattr.c
+++ b/src/ext4_xattr.c
@@ -988,13 +988,8 @@
 					    *)(list->name + name_len + 1);
 			}
 
-			/*
-			 * Size calculation by pointer arithmetics.
-			 */
-			buf_len +=
-			    (char *)((struct ext4_xattr_list_entry *)0 + 1) +
-			    name_len + 1 -
-			    (char *)(struct ext4_xattr_list_entry *)0;
+			buf_len += sizeof(struct ext4_xattr_list_entry) +
+				   name_len + 1;
 		}
 	}
 	if (list_prev)
